# The builder of the clean graph reads the number spellings of ANSI C/C99 (the grammar, section 4, #numbers). A spelling whose C99 type is int
# is the int primitive (`0x1F` is 31); any other is a value cell of its type's row of the primitive catalog, the type being the first of the
# spelling's list that holds the magnitude (`3000000000`, unsuffixed decimal beyond int and long, is llong). The dump prints the canonical
# spelling, which reads back to the same type and value: the suffix of the type (U, UL, LL, ULL, L, f), octal and hex in decimal, a floating
# value the shortest spelling that reads back, with `.0` when it would have neither point nor exponent. The native and forced images make each
# value cell again from that spelling, so the three routes print the same text.
sub: main () (
    unsigned: a 3U
    ulong: b 5UL
    llong: c 3000000000
    ullong: d 18446744073709551615ULL
    unsigned: e 0xFFFFFFFFU
    double: f 0.1
    double: g 1e3
    float: h 1.5f
    flong: k 2.5L
    size_t: m 0U
    int: x 0x1F
    long: y -5L
    double: z 0.1234567890123
    double: w 5.0
)
    int: q 1
main()
